{"event": "reasoning_response", "operation": "planner", "call_index": 1, "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Plan an executable dependency DAG of 1 to 6 tasks using only the question.\nReturn steps with question, output_slot, answer_type, inputs, execution, plus final_node_id.\nDo not answer, guess intermediate entities, or use outside knowledge. Preserve all entity,\nyear, relation and scope constraints. Ground later queries with {parent_slot} placeholders.\nexecution=retrieval obtains missing factual evidence; execution=compose combines supported\nparent conclusions and their necessary sources. Compose tasks must declare their necessary\nparents. Include the final comparison or synthesis in this same plan when needed.\nfinal_node_id identifies the existing task that answers the requested question completely.\nfinal_node_id MUST exactly equal that task's output_slot string. Never use a positional\nname such as step2 unless step2 is literally the declared output_slot. For example,\nif the final task has output_slot=\"city\", return final_node_id=\"city\".\nFor a natural chain such as director -> university -> city, city is the final task; do not\nappend a redundant answer task. The final task has no children. Independent tasks are allowed.\nUse unique simple output slots, no cycles, no hidden extra nodes. \nAlso return output_contract and program. Before ANY retrieval, plan every foreseeable\ndownstream task, its input dependencies and the designated terminal. Unknown entities,\nrules and conditions are UNBOUND INPUTS, not grounds to omit downstream tasks.\nF is the typed solving relation of this DAG; a reduced F_t never replaces the DAG.\noutput_contract has mode(value/one_reason/all_failures/semantic), result_type\n(bool/entity/number/record/set/text), entity_scope, time_scope and explanation\n(none/one/all/semantic), optional fields. Do not reduce the user's output requirements.\nprogram has variables (ID -> declaration), expression, optional constraints,\nrules_complete and rule_binding. Declaration has type, demand (a planned task ID),\noptional domain, open, unit, scope. Variables may share a task for bounded multi-field reading.\nExplicitly declare each variable's scope={entity_scope,time_scope} BEFORE retrieval,\nusing the meaning of that variable's own planned task, not the final answer's scope.\nFor example, a historical observation and a future recommendation have different time\nscopes; compared entities may differ from the final comparison's entity scope.\nThe scope of every terminal-task variable MUST equal the immutable output_contract scope.\nSupported parents retain their own scopes when used or audited by another task; never\nrelabel historical evidence as current/future evidence to satisfy the output scope.\nOmitting scope is only compatibility for old plans and inherits output_contract scope.\nOmit optional fields that do not apply; optional does not mean fill with null.\nAn unknown/unrestricted domain is represented by OMITTING domain, not null, a string,\na scalar, or an empty array. For an open entity/text variable use, for example,\n{\"type\":\"entity\",\"demand\":\"place\",\"open\":true} with no domain field.\nA known finite domain is an array of correctly typed possible values; only use\nvalues explicitly constrained by the question, never guessed answers or candidates.\nOnly number variables may use an interval domain: {\"lower\":0,\"upper\":null}.\nInterval endpoints are numbers or null (unbounded); lower_closed/upper_closed are\noptional booleans. Null endpoints are legal; a null domain itself is not.\nA numeric domain states legal possible values, NOT an acceptance threshold. Keep\nthresholds in comparison expressions, never exclude a possible failing observation.\nUnknown numeric bounds and memory-derived rules must remain unknown until sourced.\nExpressions ONLY use {op:const,value:...}, {op:var,id:...}, {op:and/or/not/eq/ne/lt/le/gt/ge/if/tuple,args:[...]},\nor {op:record/failures/one_reason,fields:{name:expression}}. No code.\nUse symbolic value mode for ordinary sourced facts/entity chains and comparisons.\nFor genuinely open advice use semantic mode, with the terminal text variable as expression.\none_reason and all_failures are ONLY Boolean-witness operators over named conditions:\none_reason requires expression={op:one_reason,fields:{name:boolean_expression,...}}\nand result_type=record; its result has exactly decision and reason. all_failures\nrequires expression={op:failures,fields:{name:boolean_expression,...}} and result_type=set;\nits result lists all failed condition names. These modes do NOT mean an ordinary\nnatural-language explanation, a historical reason, or a list of personalized reasons.\nUse value for sourced explanation text/lists, or semantic for an open synthesis.\nThe expression's declared result type must match output_contract.result_type,\nincluding an initial rule-bound placeholder; tuple expressions produce arrays (set).\nFor symbolic modes without a rule_binding completion route, rules_complete=false\nis allowed only for necessary-condition and/one_reason programs that can certify\na negative witness; it can never certify a positive or exhaustive result.\nSemantic mode is assessed from supported evidence and never receives a symbolic\ncompleteness certificate. Do not mark rules complete\nunless their completeness is justified by the task or applicable original evidence.\nrules_complete describes the completeness of the solving relation/decision rules,\nNOT corpus completeness, retrieval coverage, confidence, or whether factual input\nvalues are already known. For a direct sourced fact/entity lookup, or a fixed\ncomparison/composition whose full function is specified by the question, the\nsolving relation is complete even while all its factual inputs are still unbound:\nset rules_complete=true (or omit it). Missing facts remain unknown variables and\nrequire sources. Use false only when applicable decision rules themselves must\nstill be obtained from memory through rule_binding, or for the explicit permitted\nnegative-witness case. Never invent thresholds, source values, or completeness.\nRules from memory: do NOT invent thresholds. Plan a rule task and all foreseeable\ncondition-checking tasks with that rule as an unbound parent. Set rules_complete=false.\nrule_binding={variable:rule_variable,condition_demands:[preplanned task IDs]} permits a\nsourced rule reading to instantiate internal conditions within those tasks. Initially\nuse an unbound result variable in the expression. Keep the output contract fixed.\nEvery variable's demand must exist; parameter dependencies use the DAG inputs.\nThe terminal may be compose even when some parents will later be short-circuited.\nOptional program.task_expressions maps intermediate compose variables to whitelist\nexpressions over declared earlier DAG parents. These are free deterministic bindings;\npartial evaluation runs before demanding every planned parent. Do not use this to\ninvent sourced facts or create paid tasks. Unsupported compose remains semantic reading.\n\n\nPERSONAMEM RESPONSE-SELECTION CONTRACT (takes precedence over generic QA examples):\nThe user utterance is context for selecting a personalized conversational response.\nEven a statement such as \"I made a table\" needs an appropriate response, not merely\nthe name of an object or an event. Public candidate responses are supplied only at\nthe terminal and its audit; do not invent candidates while planning.\nPlan 1..5 useful history tasks followed by ONE semantic response-selection terminal.\nThe terminal question MUST be the exact original user utterance, unchanged. Its\nexecution is compose, with the needed history tasks as inputs. Its ONLY variable\nhas type=text; program.expression MUST reference that terminal variable. The\noutput_contract MUST be mode=semantic, result_type=text, explanation=semantic.\nThe terminal represents the response selection, not a historical fact. No symbolic certificate establishes which\npersonalized response is best. Do not use rule_binding or a deterministic\ntask_expression to replace this response selection.\nEvery nonterminal variable MUST have an explicit scope with entity_scope and\ntime_scope. Scope states whose evidence applies and when; it is not a task-role\nlabel. A source-grounded current constraint may share the response's entity and\ntime scope. Their separate variables, demands and execution phases still distinguish\nhistorical evidence from the response. Do not rename a scope merely to make it different.\nAll retrieval tasks obtain evidence in this user's conversation before the cutoff.\nNever require retrieval of external new-book catalogues, current event listings,\nnew workshop availability, or a previously executed future recommendation. Retrieve\ninterests, experiences, constraints and changes that help judge such suggestions.\nAn unrequested detail (e.g. dish name, festival name, exact packing list) must not\nbecome a mandatory prerequisite just because it is absent from the utterance.\nIntermediate compose tasks summarize sourced history; recommendations are judged\nat the terminal. Do not impose a universal exhaustive preference profile. Define\nsmall query-relevant evidence needs and bounded values rather than an entire\nhistory as one supposedly complete set. Multiple sources can jointly support a\nvalue; incomplete windows are partial evidence, not mutually exclusive whole sets.\nKeep past and later attitudes, their conditions and supporting quotations distinct.\nAn earlier dislike and later successful use may both be true; do not label the\nearlier attitude as current or apply unconditional latest-wins. The terminal must\ncompare personal fit, constraints and timeline, not just keyword or event overlap.\nEvery planned task must contribute to the designated terminal. Omit redundant tasks.\nThe current user utterance is given input. Do not require the memory to re-prove\nan event or attribute explicitly stated there. Seek the relevant prior preferences,\nexperiences and changes that contextualize it instead.\nHistory tasks need sufficient query-relevant evidence, not an exhaustive inventory\nof every past event, preference or experience. For open-ended background, ask for\na concise sourced text summary of relevant examples and changes. Use a set variable\nonly when its task explicitly defines a bounded whole set that can be established\nfrom the available history; a single witnessed member does not establish that whole.\nEvery nonterminal unrestricted text variable without a deterministic task expression\nMUST explicitly declare one of these evidence_semantics values:\n- \"personal_history_summary\": an open account of historical events, preferences,\n  experiences or changes that compatible evidence can extend. Declare this on each\n  such retrieval variable, not only on an optional later summary node. It is a\n  revisable sourced representation, not an exhaustive inventory or a unique scalar fact.\n- \"personal_atomic_fact\": one scoped atomic fact, such as a single name or date.\n  It keeps ordinary whole-value consistency and cannot use summary revision.\nDo not omit this choice or label multiple event examples or a preference trajectory\nas an atomic fact. The code will not infer a choice from the variable's name.\nNeither marker applies to numeric/rule variables, bounded domains, deterministic\ntask expressions or the final response. Each marked variable still needs its scope.\nFor an utterance such as \"I tried X\", do not make listing everything the user has\never tried a prerequisite. Retrieve the prior attitudes or experiences needed to\nunderstand X in context. A separate history-summary node is optional, not mandatory.\n\n\nFor a personalized query, relevant preferences, past experiences, dislikes,\nreasons, historical stages and constraints are evidence even when they do not repeat the new event.\nA recommendation needs evidence of the user's tastes; the history need not already contain the recommendation.\nA conversational update is not a request to discover unspecified event names, dates, locations or schedules.\nRequire these details only if actually requested. Adapt to the query: do not impose a change/reason template\non every question. Preserve uncertain cross-domain connections as implicit/partial, not explicit facts.\nDo not treat an assistant suggestion as a user preference or observation order as calendar event time.\nThis task is a personalized reply. Plan retrieval of relevant personal history, not a questionnaire for missing external facts. Keep independent history needs independent; do not block them on guessed names.\n\nReturn one complete JSON object matching this schema:\n{\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"final_node_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"output_contract\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"entity_scope\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"explanation\": {\"enum\": [\"semantic\"]}, \"fields\": {\"items\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"minItems\": 1, \"type\": \"array\", \"uniqueItems\": true}, \"mode\": {\"enum\": [\"semantic\"]}, \"result_type\": {\"enum\": [\"text\"]}, \"time_scope\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"mode\", \"result_type\", \"entity_scope\", \"time_scope\", \"explanation\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"program\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"constraints\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"object\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"expression\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Reference the sole text variable whose demand is final_node_id; history variables remain intermediate evidence.\", \"properties\": {\"id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"op\": {\"enum\": [\"var\"]}}, \"required\": [\"op\", \"id\"], \"type\": \"object\"}, \"rules_complete\": {\"type\": \"boolean\"}, \"task_expressions\": {\"additionalProperties\": {\"type\": \"object\"}, \"type